Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from the short story by Edgar Allan Poe
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. ASYLUM CELL - NIGHT

A bare stone cell drenched in deep shadow. Pale moonlight slices through iron window bars.

NARRATOR (30s), haggard with hyper-focused eyes, sits on a wooden stool, leaning forward to address an unseen listener.

NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Total darkness. A door CREAKS open an imperceptible fraction of an inch.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye!

Inside the pitch-black room, OLD MAN (70s) lies motionless in bed, sound asleep.

A hand holding a closed tin lantern reaches slowly through the narrow opening of the door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - SITTING ROOM - DAY

Bright morning sunlight fills a quiet Victorian parlor.

OLD MAN sits in a cozy wingback chair, holding a tea cup.

NARRATOR enters with a warm, cheerful smile, carrying a polished silver breakfast tray.

NARRATOR
Good morning, sir. How did you pass the night?

OLD MAN
(smiling warmly)
Ah, slept soundly, my boy. Quite soundly indeed!

NARRATOR sets down the tray, bowing politely.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Pitch dark.

The door latch turns with agonizing slowness. NARRATOR's head inches into the dark chamber.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

NARRATOR carefully unclasps the lantern shutter. A single thread of light shoots out, striking the sleeping Old Man's face.

The Old Man's eyes remain closed in sleep.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

NARRATOR gently draws back and closes the door without a sound.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The eighth night. Darkness envelopes the bedroom.

The door moves open slower than a watch's minute hand.

NARRATOR slides his head into the room. His thumb rests upon the tin latch of the lantern.

CLICK. His thumb slips on the tin fastening.

OLD MAN springs upright in bed, peering into the blackness.

OLD MAN
Who's there?

NARRATOR stands frozen in the doorway, unmoving, unbreathing.

Minutes pass in absolute silence. Neither man shifts an inch.

From the bed comes a low, stifled sound—a shivering groan of mortal dread.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief—oh, no!—it was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe.

INT. ASYLUM CELL - NIGHT

NARRATOR sits on the stool, nodding slowly in intense, terrifying empathy.

NARRATOR
I knew the sound well. Many a night, just at midnight, when all the world slept, it has welled up from my own bosom, deepening, with its dreadful echo, the terrors that distracted me. I say I knew it well.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR steadily unclasps a crevice in the lantern.

A single dim ray, thin as a spider's thread, shoots from the shutter across the room.

The light beam lands PRECISELY upon the Old Man's face.

The VULTURE EYE is open—wide, wide open. A dull blue, veiled in a hideous milky film.

NARRATOR's breath hitches. His jaw tightens in sudden fury.

A faint sound echoes in the quiet room: a low, dull, quick sound... THUMP... THUMP... THUMP... like a watch wrapped in cotton.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now, there came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The rhythmic THUMP... THUMP... THUMP quickens and grows louder.

NARRATOR grips the lantern tight, veins bulging on his forehead.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage! It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant!

The heartbeat thunders: THUMP-THUMP-THUMP-THUMP!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man's hour had come!

With a wild yell, NARRATOR throws open the lantern and LEAPS into the room!

OLD MAN
(shrieks)
No!

NARRATOR drags the Old Man to the floor, pulling the heavy bedframe and thick mattress over him.

NARRATOR leaps atop the bed, crushing the Old Man under its weight.

The muffled THUMP... THUMP... THUMP continues beneath the bed for several moments... slows... and ceases completely.

Silence falls.

NARRATOR pulls away the mattress and places his hand over the Old Man's chest.

No pulse.

NARRATOR
(whispering)
Stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR works with rapid, silent precision.

Using a crowbar, he prying up three wooden floorboards, exposing the hollow timber scantlings beneath.

He deposits the dismembered remains carefully between the scantlings.

He replaces the wooden floorboards, tapping them flush with a wooden mallet.

He holds a candle close to inspect the floor. No stain. No blood-spot.

NARRATOR catches sight of a wooden wash tub in the corner filled with red-tinted water.

NARRATOR
(softly chuckling)
A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

A sudden, echoing KNOCK... KNOCK... KNOCK rings out from the front door downstairs.

NARRATOR checks his pocket watch. The hands point to four o'clock.

INT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - HALLWAY - NIGHT

NARRATOR descends the stairs smoothly, carrying a brass oil lamp, entirely unruffled.

He reaches the front door and unlatches it with a wide, welcoming smile.

Three uniformed police officers stand on the porch: OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER CARTER, and OFFICER DAVIS.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
Good morning, sir. Police. A neighbor reported hearing a shriek during the night.

OFFICER CARTER
Suspicion of foul play was aroused, and we were deputed to search the premises.

NARRATOR
(smiling warmly)
Welcome, gentlemen! The shriek was my own—I cried out in a dream.

OFFICER DAVIS
And the old gentleman who resides here?

NARRATOR
Absent in the country! He departed just yesterday. Please, enter and search—search well!

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - DAY

Morning light filters through the shutters.

NARRATOR walks OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER CARTER, and OFFICER DAVIS into the chamber.

He opens cabinets and wooden chests, showcasing untouched gold and silver.

NARRATOR
His treasures—secure and completely undisturbed.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
Everything appears in proper order.

NARRATOR
You must be weary from your night duty! Rest here a moment.

NARRATOR grabs wooden chairs and sets them down for the officers.

Then, with casual audacity, NARRATOR places his own chair DIRECTLY over the floorboards concealing the body, and sits down comfortably.

OFFICER CARTER
Thank you, young man. A short rest is appreciated.

The three officers sit, chatting pleasantly among themselves.

OFFICER DAVIS
Quiet night across the city overall.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
Indeed. The fog cleared early.

NARRATOR smiles, nodding eagerly and chatting in a bright, friendly voice.

NARRATOR
A lovely morning! Most pleasant indeed!

Suddenly, a faint noise echoes in NARRATOR's ears.

A low, dull, quick sound... THUMP... THUMP... THUMP...

NARRATOR's smile stiffens. He twitches slightly.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears: but still they sat and still chatted. The ringing became more distinct!

NARRATOR speaks louder, making animated gestures.

NARRATOR
We must always maintain peace in the neighborhood! People are so easily alarmed by harmless sounds in the dark!

The officers nod casually, continuing their conversation.

The sound grows louder and clearer: THUMP... THUMP... THUMP... THUMP...

NARRATOR turns pale. Beads of sweat form on his temples.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I gasped for breath—and yet the officers heard it not!

NARRATOR leaps up, talking rapidly in a high key, flailing his hands.

NARRATOR
The woodwork in these old houses naturally groans and creaks! Quite natural!

The officers smile calmly, chatting without concern.

The heartbeat thunders in NARRATOR's head: THUMP! THUMP! THUMP! THUMP!

NARRATOR begins pacing back and forth across the room with heavy, violent strides.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would they not be gone? I paced the floor to and fro with heavy strides, as if excited to fury by the observations of the men—but the noise steadily increased!

NARRATOR seizes his wooden chair, slamming and scraping it violently against the floorboards!

The officers look up at him, maintaining their polite, smiling composure.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror! Anything was better than this agony!

The sound deafens him: THUMP-THUMP-THUMP-THUMP-THUMP!

NARRATOR grips his hair, eyes wide with unbearable torment. He screams at the top of his lungs!

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

The officers spring up in shock as NARRATOR collapses onto his knees, clawing frantically at the floorboards beneath him!

INT. ASYLUM CELL - NIGHT

NARRATOR sits on his stool in the dimly lit cell, breathing heavily, soaked in sweat.

He stares directly into the camera with an intense, unblinking glare.

NARRATOR
(whispering)
It is the beating of his hideous heart.

FADE OUT.

THE END
